113
00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:38.839
Parithosh Jayanthi: Yes, looks good.

114
00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:40.559
Barnabé Monnot: Okay, super now.

115
00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:48.230
Barnabé Monnot: Alright, so, faster ethereum, a quick case for quick slots in Hegota.

116
00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:58.219
Barnabé Monnot: and Barnabe from EF. As a larger point, I would say that faster ethereum is something we care about quite a lot in EF, and we see Quickslot as

117
00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:03.359
Barnabé Monnot: kind of a really important prong in making ethereum faster.

118
00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:13.270
Barnabé Monnot: So why does this matter? Like, we very strongly believe and can see that a faster ethereum is more value to our users.

119
00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:18.480
Barnabé Monnot: When you have latency in the system, that's a cost that's paid in different ways.

120
00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:33.870
Barnabé Monnot: So, if you are able to reduce latency in different, in different ways, you unlock many benefits immediately. So, some of them are here. First, you have better UX for users on the L1, with faster inclusion, so your wallet just takes faster.

121
00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:39.059
Barnabé Monnot: You have fresher prices for on-chain markets, as Vero are getting refreshed.

122
00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:41.740
Barnabé Monnot: At a higher frequency.

123
00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:54.500
Barnabé Monnot: you also get, almost for free, faster finality and a faster fast confirmation rule. Fast confirmation rule is already fast, but you can make it even faster with faster slots, so that's really, really nice.

124
00:24:54.700 --> 00:25:11.549
Barnabé Monnot: And something that's a bit underrated, but also is true, is you… because you have more leaders per second, because you have, like, more block producers per unit of time, you also increase the censorship resistance, and in particular, the economic censorship resistance, so how much it costs to keep

125
00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:15.179
Barnabé Monnot: A transaction, out of the… out of the chain.

126
00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:18.989
Barnabé Monnot: The takeaway from this, I would put it, is…

127
00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:26.959
Barnabé Monnot: It just means we… we… we have more available block space, so block space is the main thing that we are… that we are selling here.

128
00:25:27.070 --> 00:25:37.439
Barnabé Monnot: And lower Ethereum… lower latency ethereum is just more value to the network in general, and this value also does accrue to ETH, the asset.

129
00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:47.270
Barnabé Monnot: So here's, like, the quick, one-two of the proposal. The first step really is to remove

130
00:25:47.610 --> 00:26:01.520
Barnabé Monnot: The hard-coded 12-second slot assumption where it exists, so both in specifications and also client implementations, but also in smart contracts that are deployed and infrastructure that supports the chain.

131
00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:07.060
Barnabé Monnot: And then second step, Once we have removed this assumption.

132
00:26:07.270 --> 00:26:20.429
Barnabé Monnot: proceed with a moderate decrease. And here, we are targeting 10 seconds, so that would be our anchor and our proposal, but I kept it as less than or equal to 10 seconds, because I think it's also worth it to

133
00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:33.949
Barnabé Monnot: to push and see, like, how far we can go, but I… but I think anchoring on 10 seconds at this point seems to be… seems to have, like, the widest, acceptance in the community that I polled, so far.

134
00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:52.550
Barnabé Monnot: So why do this now? Why do this in Hegota? The principle I would follow here is just delivering value earlier, and I think slot time is one of these things where, once it's live, the value is almost immediately delivered. Like, you don't need to… you need to change maybe a lot of things, like, ahead of that.

135
00:26:52.580 --> 00:27:07.489
Barnabé Monnot: But once it goes live, suddenly things are faster, your AMMs refresh faster, you have more leaders, faster finality, so I think it's just really, really nice, like, it's not something that we put in place and gives us, like, benefits years from now.

136
00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:25.940
Barnabé Monnot: Additionally to that, like, I don't actually see a really good opportunity to do, like, this one-time cost of removing, like, the 12-second hard-coded assumption in the fork after Hegota, which is H-Star. It's expected that decoupled Consensus will be

137
00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:45.410
Barnabé Monnot: Or at least is currently, like, the frontrunner for that fork, and this is, like, a huge piece of work for the Consensus layer, and from informal discussions with the teams working on this, like, it's not believed that coupling this with a slot time reduction would be… would be safe, unless the work of,

138
00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:49.740
Barnabé Monnot: Reducing that, removing that assumption was done beforehand.

139
00:27:50.170 --> 00:28:04.079
Barnabé Monnot: And so, doing quick slots in H-Star means that we open the door to more gradually, like, decreasing the slot time, perhaps in I-Star, with decoupled Consensus, that gives us a little bit of headroom, and maybe again in JSTAR.

140
00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:11.349
Barnabé Monnot: So, yeah, we can just have value delivered continuously instead of no value for a long period of time.

141
00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:30.649
Barnabé Monnot: So here are a couple of considerations. So I polled the client teams, particularly the consensus-layer client teams, EF protocol teams, other people in the ecosystem. This is sort of the main technical blockers that surfaced.

142
00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:32.560
Barnabé Monnot: Through these discussions.

143
00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:41.340
Barnabé Monnot: I'll go through them one by one, but with some thoughts on where we're at with fixing or removing these blockers.

144
00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:50.249
Barnabé Monnot: The first one is, slot zero. There's a little bit more business logic happening on the Consensus layer when you move to a new epoch.

145
00:28:50.250 --> 00:29:02.129
Barnabé Monnot: So that delayed some nodes. My understanding is that this is actively discussed. There was a writer by Carlos a few months ago, and many client teams are working on optimizations already.

146
00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:22.160
Barnabé Monnot: There's, of course, the attestation and abbreviation latency, so that's one of the key bottlenecks. This is actually improved with EPBS, moving some of that out of the critical path, and then decoupled Consensus makes further headroom by reducing the size of the committee that votes on the available chain.

147
00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:35.450
Barnabé Monnot: Something that came up is, do we need to reprice data when we scale the slot time? Because right now, the data is priced constantly with how much time there is for propagation versus execution.

148
00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:51.199
Barnabé Monnot: My understanding is that it's fine either with, like, if we need to reprice, that's something we can do, and we've already set up, like, most of the testing infrastructure to figure that out. But we can also probably scale the slot time in a way that we don't need to do a repricing.

149
00:29:51.530 --> 00:29:57.289
Barnabé Monnot: In terms of contracts, many of them, or possibly many of them, are hard-coding the…

150
00:29:57.620 --> 00:30:11.949
Barnabé Monnot: the 12 seconds, so we're actually doing, like, a survey with Sourceify team on identifying this contract, and we already have, like, some of the largest contracts that might have this dependency identified, so we'll reach out to them.

151
00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:24.179
Barnabé Monnot: Same thing for infrastructures, for instance, block explorers, like, is that something that they need to pay attention to? Most likely, yes. I asked someone at Etherscan, they said, for them it's more load, but no breaking changes.

152
00:30:24.830 --> 00:30:41.630
Barnabé Monnot: testing requirements, so testing, like, at the fork, moving from 12 to, let's say, 10 seconds. I asked Parry, he seemed to say, yeah, we can do that, I mean, it's doable, and if we do decide to do it.

153
00:30:42.160 --> 00:30:49.670
Barnabé Monnot: compatibility with Decoupled Consensus. Actually, Decoupled Consensus, in my sense, was built… one of the design objectives was to

154
00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:53.939
Barnabé Monnot: move finality out of a critical path for the slot time, so I think that's actually…

155
00:30:54.380 --> 00:30:57.410
Barnabé Monnot: A good, yeah, a good thing.

156
00:30:57.990 --> 00:31:07.580
Barnabé Monnot: compatibility with ZK. There is, like, questions on the proving time in particular, because there is some fixed overhead with recursions of proofs.

157
00:31:07.640 --> 00:31:24.799
