374
00:58:13.750 --> 00:58:15.040
Jerome de Tychey: Alright.

375
00:58:19.410 --> 00:58:26.990
Jerome de Tychey: Good morning and good day, everyone. I have dropped a link to those slides in the chat. I'm here to present you EIP

376
00:58:27.210 --> 00:58:36.530
Jerome de Tychey: 8363, called Tapered Issuance Burn. There's a link to the EIP in the slides, and there's also a link to the

377
00:58:36.920 --> 00:58:41.520
Jerome de Tychey: EF Magician Tread, if you want to discuss this in, in further detail.

378
00:58:41.690 --> 00:58:50.990
Jerome de Tychey: So, as a quick overview, this proposal primary goal here is to remove the issuance mechanism's incentive for stake growth.

379
00:58:50.990 --> 00:59:03.650
Jerome de Tychey: once the staking ratio exceeds 50%. It accomplished this by deducting a burning… deducting and burning a tapered fraction of idolized Validator rewards at each epoch boundary.

380
00:59:03.720 --> 00:59:13.590
Jerome de Tychey: So that the net staking yield declines linearly, while the staking ratio, with the staking ratio, enrichs zero at 50%.

381
00:59:13.880 --> 00:59:25.549
Jerome de Tychey: So, as you may know, Ethereum has a history of regularly changing its issuance schedule, this is not the first time we are discussing an EIP to reduce issuance.

382
00:59:25.550 --> 00:59:35.939
Jerome de Tychey: Even under the proof-of-stake, regime. And, I'm very pleased to, have the stage right now to present this to you.

383
00:59:36.130 --> 00:59:41.529
Jerome de Tychey: So, why do we want to change the current curve? First, there's…

384
00:59:41.820 --> 00:59:55.690
Jerome de Tychey: A priori an endless incentive to stake, because under current issuance curve, the yield never drops below approximately 1.5%, meaning the incentive to stake, never organically switches off.

385
00:59:55.880 --> 01:00:05.039
Jerome de Tychey: Whether stake growth stops here, only depends on stakers' risk premium and the opportunity cost of other sources of yield.

386
01:00:05.250 --> 01:00:12.609
Jerome de Tychey: Those other sources of yield, the protocol can neither observe or control, and the risks

387
01:00:13.180 --> 01:00:18.610
Jerome de Tychey: The staker risk premium, keeps falling, falling as the staking infrastructure matures.

388
01:00:18.800 --> 01:00:36.929
Jerome de Tychey: Those, this endless incentive to stake has security and centralization risks, notably that high staking ratios, degrades Ethereum securities by concentrating stake. It's also creating more hazard where large operators become too big to fail.

389
01:00:37.010 --> 01:00:56.039
Jerome de Tychey: This dynamic forces independent solo stakers out due to negative dilution-adjusted returns, and furthermore, with most EF being held at custodians and staking services, the social layer, the EF that are not at stake, loses their ability to effectively coordinate a fork for social slashing.

390
01:00:56.930 --> 01:00:59.579
Jerome de Tychey: Which is our last line of defense.

391
01:00:59.890 --> 01:01:15.270
Jerome de Tychey: This also has consequence regarding the moneyness of ETH, or the role of ETH as the native money of the blockchain. The current system acts as a continuous dilution tax on unstaked ETH, forcing holders to stake simply to avoid dilution.

392
01:01:15.270 --> 01:01:27.100
Jerome de Tychey: This pushes liquid staking tokens and other staking derivatives to displace raw trustless EF as the working money of the ecosystem, which introduces counterparty smart contracts, and governance risk.

393
01:01:28.260 --> 01:01:36.240
Jerome de Tychey: So why are we proposing this for inclusion on H-star? Well, if our target is to prevent 50% at stake with current trend.

394
01:01:36.610 --> 01:01:42.120
Jerome de Tychey: The 50% of the supply at stake will likely be crossed in 2008, in 2028.

395
01:01:42.540 --> 01:01:55.100
Jerome de Tychey: So doing it later is a decision to choose a trajectory that we should avoid. Now, is the opportunity for a smooth transition towards a better issuance curve.

396
01:01:55.670 --> 01:02:07.119
Jerome de Tychey: So this is our motivation to propose this on H-star. The window closes on its own, and the reform gets harder every month, because we have more and more EF at stake.

397
01:02:07.630 --> 01:02:15.330
Jerome de Tychey: The proposed solution is the following. We are introducing a tap-and-burn function that has a 50% saturation point.

398
01:02:15.670 --> 01:02:30.340
Jerome de Tychey: This allows for a market-driven equilibrium regarding the EF at stake, and also micro-incentives remaining untouched for the behavior of the stakers, and duty-specific deductions.

399
01:02:30.540 --> 01:02:39.139
Jerome de Tychey: This has the advantage of low complexity. We have a proof-of-concept implementation from Prysm that's about 300 lines of codes.

400
01:02:39.300 --> 01:02:51.769
Jerome de Tychey: And we also have a transition plan, because if we act now, we can smooth the transition. So here you see, an animated transition for an 18 months phase-in. This is,

401
01:02:51.770 --> 01:02:59.380
Jerome de Tychey: softening the transition for the actor that will be impacted, and that will be also, two years in practice.

402
01:02:59.380 --> 01:03:11.220
Jerome de Tychey: Because between the time that we decide, to include this, EIP and the time that this EIP goes in force with HSTAR, they will likely be about 2 years.

403
01:03:12.290 --> 01:03:25.700
Jerome de Tychey: I wanted to keep this presentation short, and invite people to read the IP, as it was published quite recently. And there's also a link to the EF Magician thread, for those of you that have not seen it.

404
01:03:25.910 --> 01:03:38.809
Jerome de Tychey: The topic is obviously complex, and it's inviting intense, very intense reactions in the heat of the moment. We are going to keep on explaining and answer all the inbounds we are getting, because

405
