442
01:10:12.470 --> 01:10:22.880
Jerome de Tychey: enough for the solo stakers, and not enough for users to carry the risk of staking service providers. In which case, we are in the…

443
01:10:22.880 --> 01:10:33.720
Jerome de Tychey: based… best of both worlds. We have reduced these ones, capped, the 50% ratio, of EF at stake, and also preserved the… the…

444
01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:35.149
Jerome de Tychey: Sort of stickers.

445
01:10:35.560 --> 01:10:51.500
Jerome de Tychey: Analyzing the reservation yield is an ongoing process. We are taking feedback from the community on the matter, and we are also taking a lot of time educating people on their perception of what the reservation yield is.

446
01:10:52.360 --> 01:11:01.710
Jerome de Tychey: Behind the yield, there is the dilution adjusted yield, and there is also the taxation that you get on the yield in most jurisdictions.

447
01:11:01.870 --> 01:11:21.129
Jerome de Tychey: And we think that the burn approach is the best one to limit the impact of taxation, which, at the end of the day, creates a real yield approach for the solo stakers that's much harder for them to bear than using staking derivatives.

448
01:11:22.090 --> 01:11:39.530
Jerome de Tychey: while keeping the dilution low. So, the framing that we use is that we consider the staking yield that is actually given by the curve, corrected by what the stakers will have to pay, in terms of taxes a priori, and dilution adjusted.

449
01:11:39.850 --> 01:11:48.859
Jerome de Tychey: In which case, the tapered is the one that perceived the highest result, net result, for the longer time for the solo stakers.

