590
01:37:46.010 --> 01:37:55.870
NC: But, after that Validator is exited, the fund still remains on CL side. So, it's a really weird interaction this way.

591
01:37:58.630 --> 01:38:10.299
NC: So, just some, high-level stats today in the… on mainnet. There's still around 9,000, but they still hold the 000 credentials.

592
01:38:10.530 --> 01:38:18.109
NC: And, less than 9,000 if, you just exclude the ones that are offline for more than 6 months.

593
01:38:21.300 --> 01:38:27.690
NC: And ever since the introduction of BLS Execution Change, which is, Capella,

594
01:38:28.210 --> 01:38:35.930
NC: More than… is around more than 500,000 validators have submitted them and to convert into 0x01.

595
01:38:36.320 --> 01:38:47.309
NC: And, the key takeaway of this page is basically that, the BLS execution change, we're still seeing, two-digit usage every month.

596
01:38:47.610 --> 01:38:52.509
NC: And we are still seeing new 0x00 values being created.

597
01:38:55.860 --> 01:39:01.299
NC: So, 0x00 doesn't cause a lot of, problem today.

598
01:39:01.340 --> 01:39:15.929
NC: It's just, like, some minor, troubles. So, for example, like, protocol cannot, remove a 0x00 validator. There's no way to, withdraw that fund to anywhere.

599
01:39:16.370 --> 01:39:19.339
NC: And also the clan teams,

600
01:39:19.510 --> 01:39:30.429
NC: needs to maintain, like, the BLS execution change mechanism, like, all the gossip stuff, and the upwall, and stuff like that, they all need to maintain. And…

601
01:39:31.990 --> 01:39:45.440
NC: 000 validators, they don't submit BLS to execution change, maybe because they're lazy or ignorant, but it's also a possibility that they lost their sign, their withdrawal key.

602
01:39:45.610 --> 01:39:56.049
NC: So that they can still… they still hold the signing key to do the Validator duties, but they cannot submit the BLS to execution change to become a 0x01 Validator.

603
01:39:57.520 --> 01:39:59.459
NC: So,

604
01:39:59.670 --> 01:40:14.029
NC: why I thought about, this to, this, proposal is because when the PQ comes, every, from what I've seen so far, on the PQ, Vale Registry.

605
01:40:14.030 --> 01:40:26.440
NC: proposals. Every single one of them requires, every Validator to perform a certain action. Maybe it's to submit their XMSS key, or a certain variation of it, but

606
01:40:26.530 --> 01:40:34.520
NC: The gist of it is that there's an action required by each validator, and if that Validator doesn't act accordingly.

607
01:40:34.930 --> 01:40:39.219
NC: I think it's in the best interest, for Ethereum to eject them.

608
01:40:39.700 --> 01:40:52.910
NC: while 0x01 and 0x02 can be safely ejected and gets the ETH swept to the EL side, 0x00 doesn't have a clean way to handle this.

609
01:40:53.680 --> 01:40:59.909
NC: So, it's a very bizarre thing, because,

610
01:41:00.710 --> 01:41:10.630
NC: there's a couple approaches that I have thought about, like, how to deal with 0x00 validators, during the P2 transition. So, if we do nothing.

611
01:41:10.800 --> 01:41:13.990
NC: That means that those validators,

612
01:41:14.720 --> 01:41:20.620
NC: funds would be frozen forever, because the BLS execution change is not PQ safe.

613
01:41:20.770 --> 01:41:23.830
NC: So, there's no way, to withdraw

614
01:41:24.020 --> 01:41:28.730
NC: To convert the, validator and fast to, withdraw those funds.

615
01:41:28.960 --> 01:41:34.040
NC: Or, we just delete them from the registry, which is a very,

616
01:41:34.850 --> 01:41:43.000
NC: a brute force way to dealing with them, because it's basically, like, confiscating the funds, just, like, drop their balance from 32 ETH to 0 ETH.

617
01:41:43.640 --> 01:41:59.570
NC: And alternatively, I've also thought about, like, maybe you set up a PQ version of BLS execution change, which is a headache because you need to spend time on the research and the engineering stuff. Also, you are basically,

618
01:41:59.740 --> 01:42:16.139
NC: continue to maintain this Capella tech debt and convert into a PQ tech debt. I think PQ is a excellent opportunity to get rid of this, but if we go the other way, then we just continue to maintain this.

619
01:42:16.780 --> 01:42:27.090
NC: And the maximum usage of this PQ version of the elasticity change is just that 9,000 validators, which, in my opinion, doesn't make too much sense.

620
01:42:28.770 --> 01:42:31.770
NC: So, the proposal here, is…

621
01:42:31.890 --> 01:42:35.249
NC: Basically, first… the first step is to retire.

622
01:42:35.560 --> 01:42:41.080
NC: Which is to force exit all these, like, 0x00 validators.

623
01:42:41.540 --> 01:42:53.010
NC: So that, they will enter a stage where they exited, and their funds will still remain on the sales side. And in this stage, there's no rewards, and there's no penalty applied to them. And…

624
01:42:53.260 --> 01:42:59.810
NC: they can still unfreeze those funds by just submitting a BLS executing change. It's pretty straightforward.

625
01:43:00.410 --> 01:43:08.059
NC: And then the second step is to drain, so that, the balance, will decrease over time, slowly.

626
01:43:08.230 --> 01:43:10.000
NC: So that,

627
01:43:10.260 --> 01:43:22.019
NC: The goal is to get all of them to have a balance of zero by the time we transition to PQ, so that we don't have this, like, cliff,

628
01:43:22.130 --> 01:43:26.910
NC: that drops, 32 ETH to 0 ETH, like, at one for transition, but, like…

629
01:43:26.910 --> 01:43:36.859
Parithosh Jayanthi: Sorry to stop the presentation, but could you quickly wrap up so we still have a few minutes for questions? We're already over time, and I'd like to let people know.

630
01:43:36.860 --> 01:43:52.269
NC: This is, like, my last slide. Yeah, so basically just train them to zero so that, like, by the time PQ transition comes, it's an easier decision, for core devs to drop those validators in a safe way, because there's no more funds, remain.

631
01:43:54.250 --> 01:43:56.310
NC: And,

632
01:43:56.940 --> 01:44:10.880
NC: The entire proposal is very simple, just like one EPIP transition step, and one branch in the deposits, and then also one, function just to decrease, the balance over time.

633
01:44:11.610 --> 01:44:16.019
NC: And in exchange, we save a lot of, time.

634
01:44:16.150 --> 01:44:22.029
NC: not, needing to discuss what to do… how to deal with 0x00 validators when PQ comes.

635
01:44:24.410 --> 01:44:25.270
NC: That's it.

636
01:44:27.410 --> 01:44:35.950
Parithosh Jayanthi: Awesome, thank you. I saw a bunch of messages from potuz, Barnabas, came in. Do one of you want to speak up, or Dustin?

637
01:44:42.150 --> 01:44:43.850
Parithosh Jayanthi: Yeah, go ahead, potuz.

638
01:44:44.680 --> 01:44:56.849
potuz: I didn't understand that what do we gain from this? So, I do get the point that perhaps we want to deprecate 000, and that's an easy change, which is don't accept any more

639
01:44:57.110 --> 01:45:07.519
potuz: deposits after a while, that's a clear indication that you shouldn't be depositing validators with a zero BLS credential, but

640
01:45:07.810 --> 01:45:21.290
potuz: like, the exit, the forced exit, the churn, like, this is all complexity that doesn't get us anything. Like, with any other validator, if it becomes a problem on the post-quantum day, if they don't register, we're just going to eject them anyways.

641
01:45:21.380 --> 01:45:32.129
potuz: It's true, that money will be lost, but they… that money will be lost anyways, even with this proposal. So I don't see what do we gain by adding complexity now for something that anyways we're gonna be… end up doing.

642
01:45:32.990 --> 01:45:33.749
potuz: And we know.

643
01:45:33.750 --> 01:45:34.130
NC: No worries.

644
01:45:34.130 --> 01:45:38.309
potuz: We are going to have these validators losing that money because they just lost their keys.

645
01:45:40.270 --> 01:45:45.189
NC: But it might not be possible that they lost their keys. It might just be that they're lazy, or they're not following the program.

646
01:45:45.190 --> 01:45:54.059
potuz: I understand, but… and if they are lazy, they are not going to send a registration, and they're going to lose their money exactly the same way that this EIP gives us.

647
01:45:55.710 --> 01:46:05.220
NC: Well, then that also means that those 9,000 validators, 32 EF each, will lose all of them in one single fork transition.

648
01:46:05.850 --> 01:46:07.990
NC: Which is, in my opinion, is…

649
01:46:08.200 --> 01:46:14.479
NC: Quite a huge of a change, whereas if you drain it slowly over time, it'll be less controversial.

650
01:46:27.650 --> 01:46:42.240
Barnabas: I think the draining logic is overly complicated. I also commented on the issue, and I also commented here in the chat. I would just suggest that in H-star, we're not gonna allow any more 000 deposits.

651
01:46:42.340 --> 01:46:57.339
Barnabas: So we basically stopped dealing with those new deposits, and we just need to find a way how we can basically incentivize those remaining 9,000 validators to change their withdrawal credentials to 0x01.

652
01:46:57.500 --> 01:47:02.489
Barnabas: And I think the best way to do that is basically forcing them to exit.

653
01:47:03.820 --> 01:47:05.599
Barnabas: at H-star, as well.

654
01:47:07.250 --> 01:47:10.700
NC: Okay, so that's, like, the first EIP that I propose.

655
01:47:11.300 --> 01:47:26.710
Barnabas: Yeah, basically the first EIP in… with a combination of, basically once we hit H-star, then those Validators will be, exited, and at that point, they will still be able to change their withdrawal credential and withdraw all their ETH.

656
01:47:26.900 --> 01:47:42.059
Barnabas: Or maybe we can do that at I-Star, so that we would give them some heads up. So, like, in H-Star, we stopped accepting new deposits. In I-Star, we would basically say, when we hit I-Star, we,

657
01:47:42.060 --> 01:47:50.650
Barnabas: we exit all the validators that are still 0x00, so you would have to basically change your withdrawal credential before the Isor hit.

658
01:47:51.290 --> 01:48:04.689
NC: I don't even think you need to… you need to give them a heads up, because if… by exiting them, like, they're not… their funds are not lost at all, they're just not earning rewards, and they're just not getting penalties either.

659
01:48:04.820 --> 01:48:08.660
NC: So… I don't think, we need, like.

660
01:48:09.010 --> 01:48:13.969
NC: I don't know, 6 months, or to 1 year of noticing period for them.

661
01:48:19.600 --> 01:48:28.930
Parithosh Jayanthi: Okay, I think we should timebox the discussion for today as well. Please reach out to NC or use, ETR&D to discuss it further.

662
